        significance: 'Grace here is personified as a divine educator — it "trains" (*paideuō*: the Greek word for child-rearing, discipline, instruction). Grace is not a one-time event but an ongoing power that schools believers in godliness. This refutes any antinomian misreading of grace. The passage grounds ethical transformation in the objective event of grace appearing (the Incarnation), the future hope of Christ\'s return, and the redemptive purpose of Christ\'s self-giving. Holiness is not a condition for grace — it is grace\'s intended product.',
