        englishWord: 'assurance / substance / foundation',
        transliteration: 'hypostasis',
        definition: 'Literally "that which stands under" — the foundational substance, the underlying reality, the solid ground. In business Greek it was used for a deed of ownership or guarantee of property. In Hebrews 11:1 it describes faith as the present, substantive possession of what has been promised — as real to the one who has it as a title deed in hand.',
        strongsNumber: 'G5287',
        usage: 'Hebrews 11:1: "faith is the assurance [hypostasis] of things hoped for." Hebrews 3:14: "For we have come to share in Christ, if indeed we hold our original confidence [hypostasis] firm to the end." 2 Corinthians 9:4 and 11:17 use it for a boast that has substance behind it.',
